<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--#~
  https://www.progressiverobot.com
  Copyright (c) 2026 Christopher Holloway / Progressive Robot Ltd
  SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-or-later

  The Chocolatey package's metadata. Rendered by build/make-package-manifests.ps1,
  which substitutes every @TOKEN@; this comment is the template's own and is
  removed when it is rendered.

  The package downloads the release's own signed installer and checks it against
  the SHA-256 the manifest carries - it does not embed the 76 MB installer, which
  is why there is no tools/VERIFICATION.txt: nothing binary is shipped inside it.

  @CHOCOLATEY_ID@ is hmailserver or hmailserver-progressiverobot (the script's
  -ChocolateyId). The addresses are the project's at the group's path it moved to on
  29 September 2026; its first path, Progressiverobot/hmailserver, redirects there.

  The title, the summary and the description say that this is Progressive Robot's
  fork of hMailServer: the original hMailServer, Martin Knafve's, is developed
  again, and packages of it exist, Chocolatey's hmailserver among them, so a
  title of "hMailServer" alone would read as the original's
  (build/make-package-manifests.ps1 and build/check-package-manifests.py hold all
  three to it).

  The copyright and the authors credit the original as the installer's licence
  does (hmailserver/installation/License.rtf: "a fork of hMailServer, originally
  created by Martin Knafve, whose copyright in the original work remains"), so
  that the package page's disclaimer - that the package is not affiliated with or
  endorsed by its authors - names the original's author too, and not Progressive
  Robot alone (both scripts hold the two to it).

  The icon is the fork's own, the Control Panel's (hmailserver/source/Tools/
  ControlPanel/app.ico): its 256 by 256 frame, byte for byte as the .ico holds it,
  kept as hmailserver/source/Server/platform/packaging/hmailserver.png, which the
  Helm chart, the container image and the snap show too. iconUrl is that file on
  master, not at the version's tag: a release's package is rendered from master's
  templates (chocolatey-publish renders the latest release's, as it did 6.3.7's
  resubmission), and the tags of 6.3.7 and earlier have no such file. GitLab serves
  a raw .png as image/png, inline. build/check-package-texts.py holds the .png to
  the .ico's frame and every listing to the one address, and both scripts hold the
  rendered iconUrl to it.

  projectSourceUrl is the version's own source, the tree of its tag (@TAG@, v and
  the version, the tag every release is made from and the one releaseNotes names),
  not the project's home, which projectUrl already gives: Chocolatey's validator
  asks that it point at the software's source code, and notes that it is usually
  not projectUrl. A package is made only for a published release, so the tag
  exists when the package is pushed; both scripts hold the rendered address to it.
-->
<package xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/packaging/2015/06/nuspec.xsd">
  <metadata>
    <id>@CHOCOLATEY_ID@</id>
    <version>@VERSION@</version>
    <packageSourceUrl>https://gitlab.com/hmailserver/hmailserver/-/tree/master/hmailserver/installation/chocolatey</packageSourceUrl>
    <owners>Progressive Robot Ltd</owners>
    <title>hMailServer (Progressive Robot's fork)</title>
    <authors>Christopher Holloway, Progressive Robot Ltd (the fork), Martin Knafve (the original hMailServer)</authors>
    <projectUrl>https://gitlab.com/hmailserver/hmailserver</projectUrl>
    <iconUrl>https://gitlab.com/hmailserver/hmailserver/-/raw/master/hmailserver/source/Server/platform/packaging/hmailserver.png</iconUrl>
<!--#~ REUSE-IgnoreStart: the package's copyright field, which reuse would read as this file's notice -->
    <copyright>Copyright (c) 2026 Progressive Robot Ltd. A fork of hMailServer, originally created by Martin Knafve, whose copyright in the original work remains.</copyright>
<!--#~ REUSE-IgnoreEnd -->
    <licenseUrl>https://gitlab.com/hmailserver/hmailserver/-/blob/master/LICENSE</licenseUrl>
    <requireLicenseAcceptance>false</requireLicenseAcceptance>
    <projectSourceUrl>https://gitlab.com/hmailserver/hmailserver/-/tree/@TAG@</projectSourceUrl>
    <docsUrl>https://gitlab.com/hmailserver/hmailserver/-/wikis/home</docsUrl>
    <bugTrackerUrl>https://gitlab.com/hmailserver/hmailserver/-/issues</bugTrackerUrl>
    <tags>hmailserver mail mailserver email smtp imap pop3 dkim dmarc spam webmail server admin</tags>
    <summary>Progressive Robot's fork of hMailServer, not the original: a free, open-source mail server for Windows - SMTP, IMAP and POP3, with anti-spam and anti-virus filtering, DKIM, DMARC, MTA-STS, a webmail client and a REST API.</summary>
    <description><![CDATA[
This is Progressive Robot's fork of hMailServer, not the original. The original hMailServer is
Martin Knafve's; it is developed separately and has releases of its own, and packages of it exist,
Chocolatey's hmailserver among them. Progressive Robot Ltd forked it in 2026 and released 6.0.0,
the fork's first release, on 12 June 2026.

hMailServer is a free, open-source mail server implementing SMTP, IMAP and POP3, with a Windows
administration program, a browser administration console, a webmail client and a REST API. The
fork brings a current toolchain, current cryptography, and current transport-security and
authentication standards, and upgrades an installation of the original up to 5.6 in place, with a
continuous database upgrade chain from each of its versions. It does not upgrade the original's 5.7
builds of August and September 2026: its database upgrade has no step from their database versions,
5709 (the pre-release builds 2788 and 2843) and 5711 (5.7.0 build 3013, 5.7.1 and 5.7.2). Both
installers register under the same uninstall entry, hMailServer_is1, so on a machine
where the original is installed this package's installer finds that installation as its own: it
upgrades the original up to 5.6; from the release after 6.3.7 it refuses the original's 5.7 before
it changes anything (exit code 1, the reason in %TEMP%\hMailServer-setup-refused.txt), and
the installers of 6.3.7 and earlier do not look, so do not install one of them over the original's
5.7 - move a 5.7 server's mail over IMAP instead. The database upgrade is one-way: take a backup
first.

### What it does

SMTP with submission and implicit TLS, IMAP4rev1 with the extensions a modern client asks for, POP3,
ManageSieve, SPF, DKIM signing and verification, DMARC with reporting, MTA-STS and TLS-RPT, DANE,
ARC, SpamAssassin and ClamAV integration, greylisting, DNS blacklists and SURBL, auto-ban,
two-factor authentication for administrators and accounts, application passwords, OAuth 2.0
(inbound and outbound, including Microsoft 365), CardDAV address books, full-text search,
quarantine, archiving and legal holds, backup and restore, Prometheus metrics and OpenTelemetry
export.

### What this package does

It downloads the release's Authenticode-signed installer from the project's release on GitLab,
checks it against the SHA-256 recorded in this package, and runs it unattended. That installs
the server, the Control Panel and the setup tools, and configures the embedded database.

An unattended install leaves the administrator password unset, because the wizard page that asks
for one is skipped. Set one before the server is reachable from anywhere but the machine itself:
open the Control Panel from the Start menu.

Uninstalling runs the installer's own uninstaller. It does not delete the message store or the
database: that is deliberate, and removing them is the administrator's decision to make by hand.
]]></description>
    <releaseNotes>https://gitlab.com/hmailserver/hmailserver/-/releases/@TAG@</releaseNotes>
  </metadata>
  <files>
    <file src="tools\**" target="tools" />
  </files>
</package>
